   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the transportation law, in relation to regulating the operation of motor vehicles engaged in the non-emergency transport of passengers for medical purposes",
    "basePrintNo": "S7799",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7799-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §160, Transp L",
    "lawSection": "Transportation Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7799\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the transportation law, in relation to regulating the\noperation of motor vehicles engaged in the non-emergency transport of\npassengers for medical purposes\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to help ensure the safe transport of\nnon-ambulatory individuals that are being transported on a\nnon-emergency basis to, from or between any health care facility.\nCurrently, while there are credible safety standards for the transport\nof patients or individuals in an emergency situation by ambulance,\nthere are few safeguards for those individuals that are being\ntransported on a non-emergency basis. This is primarily the case,\nbecause during emergency transports, there is an EMT or other health\ncare professional that is supervising care of the individual during\ntransport. However, during non-emergency transports there is no such\ncertified emergency medical personnel.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1: Adds a new Transportation Law section 160 to help ensure\nthe safe transport of non-ambulatory individuals that are being\ntransported on a non-emergency basis to, from or between any health\ncare facility. This provision helps to make such non-emergency trips\nconducted by ambulances more safe in the following ways. First, it\nrestricts the period of time that any person may operate a\nnon-emergency medical transport vehicle and the number of hours of\nduty for each driver shift. The standards will be the same as is the\ncase for other drivers regulated by Transportation Law section 211.\n\nIn addition, in consultation with the state emergency medical services\ncouncil, the Department shall recommend such training that a driver of\na non-emergency medical transport vehicle shall have when transporting\nan individual and there is no certified emergency medical personnel on\nboard during such transport. Such training shall include care of\npatients on a non-emergency basis, securing non-ambulatory patients\nwithin such vehicle, and actions to be taken in the event of an\nemergency from either the patient or the vehicle.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\n120 days after it shall have become law.\n",
